1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,000
Music

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Classic toys like electric trains,

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
squishy, colorful Play-Doh,

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
the pinball machine, or even a slinky

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
are the kinds of toys that stand the test of time.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
It all evokes nostalgia and memories of a simpler time

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
and are impervious to trends.

8
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:32,500
Last year, Americans spent more than $25 billion on toys.

9
00:00:32,501 --> 00:00:34,800
Toys are beacons of our childhood,

10
00:00:34,801 --> 00:00:37,840
and that's why full-grown adults will spend a whole lot of time

11
00:00:37,841 --> 00:00:41,640
and even more money trying to purchase a piece of their youth.

12
00:00:41,641 --> 00:00:43,760
I got my doll!

13
00:00:43,761 --> 00:00:45,760
Some of the most iconic toy brands

14
00:00:45,761 --> 00:00:48,360
have been in business for decades.

15
00:00:48,361 --> 00:00:51,200
And today, we've got all-access passes

16
00:00:51,201 --> 00:00:56,200
to one of the most iconic toys, Play-Doh.

17
00:00:56,201 --> 00:00:58,240
What is it really made out of?

18
00:00:58,241 --> 00:01:02,200
And how do they make 108,000 cans

19
00:01:02,201 --> 00:01:05,540
of colorful, sweet-smelling Play-Doh per day?

20
00:01:05,541 --> 00:01:08,380
Plus, we'll see how a plastic manufacturer

21
00:01:08,381 --> 00:01:11,880
prepares classic toy soldiers for battle.

22
00:01:11,881 --> 00:01:14,720
We'll also get an inside look at pinball machines

23
00:01:14,721 --> 00:01:17,180
and discover why they were once banned

24
00:01:17,181 --> 00:01:19,220
in cities across America.

25
00:01:19,221 --> 00:01:22,600
But all work and no play make Adam a dull boy.

26
00:01:22,601 --> 00:01:24,520
Today, I want to have some fun.

27
00:01:24,521 --> 00:01:27,520
This is Modern Marvel's Classic Toys.

28
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:43,240
Here at the Cardamundi factory in East Laumetown, Massachusetts,

29
00:01:43,241 --> 00:01:46,080
they're making something so special

30
00:01:46,081 --> 00:01:48,440
that just mention its name anywhere in the world

31
00:01:48,441 --> 00:01:51,040
and someone is sure to crack a smile.

32
00:01:51,041 --> 00:01:53,040
Play-Doh!

33
00:01:53,041 --> 00:01:55,580
That crack and pop when you open the canister,

34
00:01:55,581 --> 00:01:57,380
that distinctive smell.

35
00:01:57,381 --> 00:01:59,040
Oh, man.

36
00:01:59,041 --> 00:02:00,840
Even through the mask.

37
00:02:00,841 --> 00:02:03,220
You can use it to make a beautiful work of art

38
00:02:03,221 --> 00:02:05,220
or a beautiful mess.

39
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:08,280
Believe it or not,

40
00:02:08,281 --> 00:02:11,860
Play-Doh was actually in our homes for decades

41
00:02:11,861 --> 00:02:14,720
before it was ever seen as a plaything.

42
00:02:14,721 --> 00:02:17,200
So in 1933,

43
00:02:17,201 --> 00:02:21,360
Play-Doh was actually developed to be a wallpaper cleaner.

44
00:02:21,361 --> 00:02:25,600
And then over the years, the wallpaper technology changed so much

45
00:02:25,601 --> 00:02:30,200
that Play-Doh was really not required to be a wallpaper cleaner.

46
00:02:30,201 --> 00:02:36,200
By 1956, Joseph McVicker had reimagined the pliable cleaning substance.

47
00:02:36,201 --> 00:02:41,020
Thanks to his sister-in-law, Kay Zuhal, who's a school teacher,

48
00:02:41,021 --> 00:02:44,360
actually took Play-Doh to her classroom,

49
00:02:44,361 --> 00:02:47,920
and she was amazed with the way children were using Play-Doh

50
00:02:47,921 --> 00:02:49,960
and expressing their creativity.

51
00:02:49,961 --> 00:02:52,500
And that's really how Play-Doh transformed

52
00:02:52,501 --> 00:02:55,800
to being a toy that it is today.

53
00:02:55,801 --> 00:02:58,640
As of 2021, the year that Play-Doh celebrates

54
00:02:58,641 --> 00:03:00,680
its 65th anniversary,

55
00:03:00,681 --> 00:03:05,800
they have sold 4.5 billion cans.

56
00:03:05,801 --> 00:03:09,020
Last year alone, they shipped the same weighted product

57
00:03:09,021 --> 00:03:13,420
as the Statue of Liberty and the Eiffel Tower combined.

58
00:03:13,421 --> 00:03:16,720
We love it, we're familiar with it, but what's in it?

59
00:03:16,721 --> 00:03:18,360
How do they make Play-Doh?

60
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:24,960
Hey, Jeremy, I gotta tell you, I love the smell.

61
00:03:24,961 --> 00:03:28,960
You walk into this room and you're immediately transported

62
00:03:28,961 --> 00:03:29,960
into your childhood.

63
00:03:29,961 --> 00:03:30,960
Yeah.

64
00:03:30,961 --> 00:03:33,960
How many cans of Play-Doh are you selling per year?

65
00:03:33,961 --> 00:03:35,960
We're doing about 25 million cans per year,

66
00:03:35,961 --> 00:03:39,960
108,000 cans per day, about 290 cans per minute.

67
00:03:39,961 --> 00:03:41,960
It's crazy amounts that we're doing right here.

68
00:03:41,961 --> 00:03:44,960
I'm trying to wrap my head around this.

69
00:03:44,961 --> 00:03:45,960
Look at this.

70
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:50,960
Look at this.

71
00:03:50,961 --> 00:03:52,960
It's like if there was a little Play-Doh village,

72
00:03:52,961 --> 00:03:54,960
and this is where they all boarded the train.

73
00:03:54,961 --> 00:03:56,960
Jeremy, please unlock the secrets of my childhood.

74
00:03:56,961 --> 00:03:58,960
Tell me how they make Play-Doh, please.

75
00:03:58,961 --> 00:04:00,960
First, we want to put everything into the mixer,

76
00:04:00,961 --> 00:04:01,960
and we do that right here.

77
00:04:01,961 --> 00:04:05,960
We have silos outside, and we get deliveries once a week,

78
00:04:05,961 --> 00:04:07,960
and we run about 10,000 pounds a day.

79
00:04:07,961 --> 00:04:09,960
Just like any dough,

80
00:04:09,961 --> 00:04:12,960
Play-Doh has dry and wet ingredients

81
00:04:12,961 --> 00:04:15,360
that are fed through separate channels

82
00:04:15,361 --> 00:04:17,300
down into a mixing chamber.

83
00:04:17,301 --> 00:04:19,700
The ingredients are deployed not only

84
00:04:19,701 --> 00:04:23,400
in specific measurements, but in a specific sequence.

85
00:04:23,401 --> 00:04:27,000
The order of deployment is designed to ensure

86
00:04:27,001 --> 00:04:30,580
that every ingredient is distributed evenly

87
00:04:30,581 --> 00:04:32,020
within the mixer.

88
00:04:32,021 --> 00:04:34,980
And mixing up the secret formula for today's batch

89
00:04:34,981 --> 00:04:36,780
is operator Troy.

90
00:04:36,781 --> 00:04:38,360
First step is flour here.

91
00:04:38,361 --> 00:04:39,061
Yes, sir.

92
00:04:39,062 --> 00:04:40,980
In our hopper.

93
00:04:40,981 --> 00:04:44,020
Now, the flour is going down.

94
00:04:44,021 --> 00:04:46,020
We use 615 pounds per batch.

95
00:04:46,021 --> 00:04:48,020
How many batches are you going through in a day?

96
00:04:48,021 --> 00:04:50,020
We use about 18 batches a day.

97
00:04:50,021 --> 00:04:52,020
We use about a ton and a half of...

98
00:04:52,021 --> 00:04:54,020
Of flour? Yes.

99
00:04:54,021 --> 00:04:56,020
That much flour could make

100
00:04:56,021 --> 00:04:58,740
more than 5,000 average batches of cookies,

101
00:04:58,741 --> 00:05:02,740
which would amount to more than 300,000 cookies.

102
00:05:02,741 --> 00:05:06,100
Once the hopper empties all of the flour into the mixer,

103
00:05:06,101 --> 00:05:09,140
it's time to add the other ingredients.

104
00:05:09,141 --> 00:05:10,980
That's incredible.

105
00:05:10,981 --> 00:05:12,640
Is that suction?

106
00:05:12,641 --> 00:05:14,480
Yeah.

107
00:05:14,481 --> 00:05:16,980
I need those for soil in my garden.

108
00:05:16,981 --> 00:05:19,480
While Play-Doh is certified non-toxic,

109
00:05:19,481 --> 00:05:22,480
it also contains chemical preservatives

110
00:05:22,481 --> 00:05:24,480
and a high dose of salt.

111
00:05:24,481 --> 00:05:26,980
It isn't intended to be eaten,

112
00:05:26,981 --> 00:05:29,480
but it is still a wheat-based dough

113
00:05:29,481 --> 00:05:31,980
of infinite creativity waiting to happen.

114
00:05:31,981 --> 00:05:34,980
Among the dry ingredients that are added into the mixer

115
00:05:34,981 --> 00:05:39,480
are cornstarch, dry pigment, two types of salt,

116
00:05:39,481 --> 00:05:43,080
salt, and some top secret preservatives.

117
00:05:43,081 --> 00:05:44,920
And some of the key wet ingredients

118
00:05:44,921 --> 00:05:47,660
include liquid pigment, hot water,

119
00:05:47,661 --> 00:05:52,420
and of course, the element that takes us back to our childhoods.

120
00:05:52,421 --> 00:05:54,560
This is the ingredient that every child loves.

121
00:05:54,561 --> 00:05:57,140
Once they open it up, you know, the can of Play-Doh,

122
00:05:57,141 --> 00:05:59,960
this is what they smell here.

123
00:05:59,961 --> 00:06:00,800
That's it?

124
00:06:00,801 --> 00:06:01,800
Wait, wait, wait, one second.

125
00:06:01,801 --> 00:06:03,100
Just a little.

126
00:06:04,980 --> 00:06:06,800
Oh, it smells delightful.

127
00:06:06,801 --> 00:06:09,400
And with that little tiny couple of fragrance?

128
00:06:09,401 --> 00:06:10,101
Yep.

129
00:06:10,102 --> 00:06:11,300
Gives you all the smell you need.

130
00:06:11,301 --> 00:06:12,001
That's crazy.

131
00:06:12,002 --> 00:06:12,702
Beautiful, isn't it?

132
00:06:12,703 --> 00:06:13,900
That's crazy.

133
00:06:13,901 --> 00:06:17,040
This sweet, flowery, vanilla-like fragrance

134
00:06:17,041 --> 00:06:20,640
has slight overtones of cherry and the natural smell

135
00:06:20,641 --> 00:06:22,140
of a wheat-based dough.

136
00:06:22,141 --> 00:06:24,460
And it's added purely for fragrance,

137
00:06:24,461 --> 00:06:27,860
because that is what helps identify and distinguish

138
00:06:27,861 --> 00:06:29,660
the iconic Play-Doh.

139
00:06:29,661 --> 00:06:33,300
Now the dough is ready to be mixed.

140
00:06:33,301 --> 00:06:36,000
I'm going to take the start back.

141
00:06:36,001 --> 00:06:37,540
That was coming down for 15 minutes.

142
00:06:37,541 --> 00:06:39,960
15 minutes and Play-Doh's gonna pop out on that line?

143
00:06:39,961 --> 00:06:40,960
Yes.

144
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:43,160
No way.

145
00:06:45,160 --> 00:06:47,040
What do you think it is about Play-Doh

146
00:06:47,041 --> 00:06:48,960
that makes it so timeless?

147
00:06:48,961 --> 00:06:50,340
I think it's adaptable.

148
00:06:50,341 --> 00:06:53,040
It's up to your creativity to do what you want with it.

149
00:06:53,041 --> 00:06:56,040
It's up for the kids to make up their own mind with it.

150
00:06:56,041 --> 00:06:58,840
Play-Doh isn't alone in inspiring kids

151
00:06:58,841 --> 00:07:00,600
to use their imagination.

152
00:07:00,601 --> 00:07:03,000
There's another classic toy that allows us

153
00:07:03,001 --> 00:07:05,040
to build whatever we imagine,

154
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:08,200
from cherished monuments to entire cities,

155
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:10,160
one block at a time.

156
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:13,160
Lego got started in the early 1900s

157
00:07:13,780 --> 00:07:16,060
in a carpentry and woodworking shop

158
00:07:16,061 --> 00:07:18,760
owned by Ole Kirk Christensen.

159
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:21,200
Christensen named his company

160
00:07:21,201 --> 00:07:23,120
by shortening the Danish phrase,

161
00:07:23,540 --> 00:07:26,140
which means play well.

162
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:29,420
When he finally got a hold of plastics,

163
00:07:29,421 --> 00:07:31,760
he realized that he could mold them into shapes.

164
00:07:31,761 --> 00:07:35,760
And in 1946, he bought his first injection molding machine.

165
00:07:35,761 --> 00:07:39,760
By 1958, the first LEGO bricks were released.

166
00:07:39,761 --> 00:07:43,760
And all these years later, the original 1958 LEGO bricks

167
00:07:43,761 --> 00:07:46,760
will connect to any of the bricks that are made today.

168
00:07:46,761 --> 00:07:50,760
That accuracy in manufacturing is what makes LEGO

169
00:07:50,761 --> 00:07:53,760
one of the most classic toys ever.

170
00:07:53,761 --> 00:07:58,760
It also means they can be handed down from generation to generation.

171
00:07:58,761 --> 00:08:05,760
Between 1958 and 2008, they made 400 billion bricks of LEGO,

172
00:08:05,761 --> 00:08:09,760
which means 10 people can actually make LEGO towers

173
00:08:09,761 --> 00:08:13,760
that are 40 billion pieces from here to the moon.

174
00:08:14,760 --> 00:08:19,760
LEGOs recently released their largest set to date,

175
00:08:19,761 --> 00:08:22,760
the LEGO Art World Map.

176
00:08:22,761 --> 00:08:29,660
With over 11,600 pieces, it's 2,500 more pieces

177
00:08:29,661 --> 00:08:35,480
than their previously largest set, the Architect Colosseum.

178
00:08:35,481 --> 00:08:39,400
One of the largest LEGO structures ever built

179
00:08:39,401 --> 00:08:44,040
is a full-scale LEGO Star Wars X-Wing fighter model.

180
00:08:44,041 --> 00:08:49,320
It took 32 builders more than 17,000 hours

181
00:08:49,321 --> 00:08:53,040
to put together over 5 million LEGO bricks.

182
00:08:53,041 --> 00:08:55,820
That's close to two years.

183
00:08:55,821 --> 00:09:00,440
This life-sized replica is 42 times the size

184
00:09:00,441 --> 00:09:04,580
and weighs more than 45,000 pounds.

185
00:09:04,581 --> 00:09:08,380
That's more than some actual fighter jets weigh.

186
00:09:08,381 --> 00:09:12,660
From LEGO roller coaster sets to LEGO space shuttles,

187
00:09:12,661 --> 00:09:15,860
there's an entire universe to reimagine.

188
00:09:15,861 --> 00:09:19,060
And there's another classic toy that can make us believe

189
00:09:19,061 --> 00:09:22,600
we are our own heroes of the battlefield.

190
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:30,820
Little plastic soldiers have existed since the late 1930s

191
00:09:30,821 --> 00:09:34,960
and allowed kids to feel like they were their own World War II heroes,

192
00:09:35,320 --> 00:09:40,100
defeating the enemy and conquering make-believe battlefields.

193
00:09:41,080 --> 00:09:44,040
I'm Jeff Immel, and I'm the president of BMC Toys.

194
00:09:44,380 --> 00:09:46,100
We make new toys from old molds

195
00:09:46,101 --> 00:09:48,420
and bring them to a whole new generation to enjoy.

196
00:09:48,421 --> 00:09:50,980
Little Green Army men were inducted into the National

197
00:09:50,981 --> 00:09:53,420
Toy Hall of Fame in 2014.

198
00:09:53,421 --> 00:09:55,260
If you say Little Green Army men,

199
00:09:55,261 --> 00:09:57,020
everybody knows what you're talking about.

200
00:09:57,021 --> 00:09:59,460
It's just become a ubiquitous part of childhood

201
00:09:59,461 --> 00:10:01,060
and our pop culture.

202
00:10:01,061 --> 00:10:07,340
In the 1990s, BMC Toys acquired molds from the 50s and 60s

203
00:10:07,341 --> 00:10:11,540
to produce these historic plastic army sets.

204
00:10:11,541 --> 00:10:15,940
BMC Toys then teamed up with Northeast Plastics

205
00:10:15,941 --> 00:10:18,740
to operate the mid-century molds.

206
00:10:18,741 --> 00:10:21,240
Everything that you're seeing on the shelf right now

207
00:10:21,241 --> 00:10:25,780
is gonna be original dyes made for Army figures,

208
00:10:25,781 --> 00:10:27,580
vehicles, things of that nature,

209
00:10:27,581 --> 00:10:30,880
from the, you know, 50s, 60s, and a little bit of the 70s.

210
00:10:30,881 --> 00:10:33,160
The molds are owned by BMC Toys.

211
00:10:33,161 --> 00:10:36,160
They have over 70 molds with us currently.

212
00:10:36,161 --> 00:10:39,000
And it's still here living and breathing and functioning.

213
00:10:39,001 --> 00:10:40,340
It's pretty impressive, you know?

214
00:10:40,341 --> 00:10:41,760
They were making stuff back then

215
00:10:41,761 --> 00:10:44,200
that really stood the test of time.

216
00:10:44,201 --> 00:10:45,960
This is the injection molding department,

217
00:10:45,961 --> 00:10:48,880
so this is where we're manufacturing the plastic parts.

218
00:10:48,881 --> 00:10:53,440
We run over 70 molds of different Army configurations,

219
00:10:53,441 --> 00:10:57,020
20,000, 30,000 shots of this a year.

220
00:10:57,021 --> 00:10:59,240
But those molds from the mid-century

221
00:10:59,241 --> 00:11:02,520
never included plastic Army women.

222
00:11:02,521 --> 00:11:06,320
That is, until Jeff Immel and BMC Toys

223
00:11:06,321 --> 00:11:08,860
decided to do it in 2020.

224
00:11:08,861 --> 00:11:11,700
Soon after I began selling plastic Army men,

225
00:11:11,701 --> 00:11:13,620
I got a letter from a little girl saying, hey,

226
00:11:13,621 --> 00:11:15,780
why don't we make girl army men?

227
00:11:15,781 --> 00:11:17,820
About 18 months later,

228
00:11:17,821 --> 00:11:21,260
BMC Toys released a new line of army women,

229
00:11:21,261 --> 00:11:25,040
featuring a female captain holding a handgun and binoculars,

230
00:11:25,041 --> 00:11:27,960
a kneeling female soldier calling in an air strike,

231
00:11:27,961 --> 00:11:29,840
among 30 other poses.

232
00:11:29,841 --> 00:11:31,380
They come in three colors,

233
00:11:31,381 --> 00:11:34,800
classic army green, tan, and pink.

234
00:11:37,580 --> 00:11:40,620
Classic toys, like little plastic soldiers,

235
00:11:40,621 --> 00:11:44,160
are not gender-specific or era-specific.

236
00:11:44,161 --> 00:11:49,220
Every new generation of kids inspires a love of classic toys.

237
00:11:49,221 --> 00:11:52,300
Some are so popular they have their very own day,

238
00:11:52,301 --> 00:11:54,000
like Play-Doh.

239
00:11:54,001 --> 00:11:56,240
Every year on September 16th,

240
00:11:56,241 --> 00:11:59,040
Americans celebrate this classic toy

241
00:11:59,041 --> 00:12:02,400
that has reportedly sold upwards of four billion cans

242
00:12:02,401 --> 00:12:05,080
since its debut in 1956.

243
00:12:05,081 --> 00:12:07,820
And we're at their manufacturing facilities

244
00:12:07,821 --> 00:12:10,620
in East Longmeadow, Massachusetts.

245
00:12:10,621 --> 00:12:13,040
they're cranking out the colorful dough

246
00:12:13,041 --> 00:12:15,340
to keep up with the continued demand.

247
00:12:15,341 --> 00:12:18,160
All right, how does it go from being made

248
00:12:18,161 --> 00:12:20,080
to coming to our store?

249
00:12:20,081 --> 00:12:22,300
So, Charlie, it's gonna be coming through our mixer,

250
00:12:22,301 --> 00:12:23,800
out onto this first conveyor.

251
00:12:23,801 --> 00:12:26,260
That's where our quality assurance comes into effect,

252
00:12:26,261 --> 00:12:27,740
and they're gonna take the temperature on that,

253
00:12:27,741 --> 00:12:29,740
make sure we're hitting the right marks.

254
00:12:29,741 --> 00:12:34,060
What is the ideal temperature for freshly mixed Play-Doh?

255
00:12:34,061 --> 00:12:36,520
It's warmer than your average hot shower.

256
00:12:36,521 --> 00:12:37,360
Why?

257
00:12:37,361 --> 00:12:39,200
The mixer doesn't bake it.

258
00:12:39,201 --> 00:12:41,400
It's the hot water that makes it go through the process

259
00:12:41,401 --> 00:12:42,960
to turn it into dough.

260
00:12:42,961 --> 00:12:45,540
So it's residual heat from the hot water.

261
00:12:45,541 --> 00:12:46,340
Yeah.

262
00:12:46,341 --> 00:12:47,160
Got it.

