00:22:00,641 --> 00:22:02,780
Actually, they use a light table that we have over here

452
00:22:02,781 --> 00:22:04,080
for inspecting the boards.

453
00:22:04,081 --> 00:22:05,640
Wow.

454
00:22:05,641 --> 00:22:09,160
These color inserts are what flash and light up,

455
00:22:09,161 --> 00:22:13,360
hinting to the player where to aim and how to score big.

456
00:22:13,361 --> 00:22:15,860
But the flashing lights are also the thing

457
00:22:15,861 --> 00:22:19,300
that attract a person to insert the coin.

458
00:22:19,301 --> 00:22:23,200
But not all classic toys require flashing lights

459
00:22:23,201 --> 00:22:24,700
to be entertaining.

460
00:22:24,701 --> 00:22:28,380
Some are just as amusing and just as classic

461
00:22:28,381 --> 00:22:32,020
using only one long metal wire.

462
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:37,180
Classic toys often do not need batteries

463
00:22:37,181 --> 00:22:41,420
or bells and whistles to tantalize young or old minds.

464
00:22:41,421 --> 00:22:46,460
In fact, there's one toy that has been around for over 75 years

465
00:22:46,461 --> 00:22:49,420
with over 300 million sold.

466
00:22:49,421 --> 00:22:52,640
And it's only made up of one part.

467
00:22:52,641 --> 00:22:54,460
It makes the happiest sound.

468
00:22:54,461 --> 00:22:55,740
Everyone knows it's Slinky.

469
00:22:59,740 --> 00:23:02,600
This is what we make the Slinky, along with the Shrinky Dinks

470
00:23:02,601 --> 00:23:04,980
and the Spinwell.

471
00:23:04,981 --> 00:23:06,820
We still use some of the older equipment

472
00:23:06,821 --> 00:23:09,040
we used back in 1945.

473
00:23:09,041 --> 00:23:10,140
Let me show you around.

474
00:23:11,420 --> 00:23:14,820
This is our spool wire.

475
00:23:14,821 --> 00:23:17,420
They put 1,600 pounds on a spool,

476
00:23:17,421 --> 00:23:20,420
which makes approximately 3,200 Slinkys.

477
00:23:20,421 --> 00:23:23,420
At Slinkys' sole manufacturing facility,

478
00:23:23,421 --> 00:23:28,420
they're making approximately 25,000 to 30,000 Slinkys daily.

479
00:23:28,421 --> 00:23:31,420
The yearly production is probably close to around 2 million.

480
00:23:31,421 --> 00:23:33,420
After we set the spool in place,

481
00:23:33,421 --> 00:23:35,420
the wire is fed up through the machine

482
00:23:35,421 --> 00:23:37,420
and then right through the straightener.

483
00:23:37,421 --> 00:23:39,420
Then it goes up to the flattening mill,

484
00:23:39,421 --> 00:23:41,420
which is where the magic happens,

485
00:23:41,421 --> 00:23:43,420
and that's where he's given its coil.

486
00:23:43,421 --> 00:23:45,420
There's a lot of classic toys

487
00:23:45,421 --> 00:23:47,420
that were born completely by accident,

488
00:23:47,421 --> 00:23:49,420
and Slinky is one of them.

489
00:23:49,421 --> 00:23:52,420
Richard James was in the Navy, and he had springs on a table,

490
00:23:52,421 --> 00:23:55,420
which were designed to keep equipment from breaking.

491
00:23:55,421 --> 00:23:58,420
Well, one of those springs fell off the table,

492
00:23:58,421 --> 00:24:00,420
and then it just, like, dropped down onto a book

493
00:24:00,421 --> 00:24:01,420
and then onto the floor, and he thought,

494
00:24:01,421 --> 00:24:03,420
that's pretty amazing.

495
00:24:03,421 --> 00:24:05,420
By the 1945 holiday season,

496
00:24:05,421 --> 00:24:09,420
Slinky was introduced at Gimbel's department store.

497
00:24:09,421 --> 00:24:12,420
When it debuted at the department store in Philadelphia,

498
00:24:12,421 --> 00:24:13,420
it was a huge hit.

499
00:24:13,421 --> 00:24:16,420
They sold 400 units, every one they had,

500
00:24:16,421 --> 00:24:18,420
in less than 90 minutes.

501
00:24:18,421 --> 00:24:21,420
Back then, a Slinky cost $1,

502
00:24:21,421 --> 00:24:24,420
about $14 in today's value.

503
00:24:24,421 --> 00:24:27,420
This is where the Slinky gets cut here

504
00:24:27,421 --> 00:24:29,420
into a specific length.

505
00:24:29,421 --> 00:24:31,420
It comes down the strip,

506
00:24:31,421 --> 00:24:33,420
then the operator puts a crimp on it.

507
00:24:33,421 --> 00:24:36,920
The crimp is there for safety reasons.

508
00:24:36,921 --> 00:24:38,920
Once an hour, we pull slinkies off the belt,

509
00:24:38,921 --> 00:24:40,420
and then we make sure it stands up,

510
00:24:40,421 --> 00:24:43,420
and we check the crimps to make sure the crimps are tight.

511
00:24:43,421 --> 00:24:45,760
You stretch it out, it's about 8 feet long,

512
00:24:45,761 --> 00:24:48,260
but there's 81 feet of wire here.

513
00:24:48,261 --> 00:24:50,680
We have to make sure we hear the sound of the slinky.

514
00:24:52,680 --> 00:24:56,180
Every hour, approximately 1,500 slinkies

515
00:24:56,181 --> 00:24:59,420
travel down this conveyor belt to be boxed.

516
00:24:59,421 --> 00:25:02,780
That means every 2.4 seconds,

517
00:25:02,781 --> 00:25:05,580
a Slinky slides into its box.

518
00:25:05,581 --> 00:25:07,780
Along with the classic Slinky,

519
00:25:07,781 --> 00:25:10,780
they also produce the giant Slinky,

